{
  "id": "present-simple-he-she-it",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "Every day",
  "title": "Present simple: he, she, it and the -s",
  "tagline": "The one small letter that trips everyone up: he, she, it — and the verb gets an -s.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Speaking Part 1 — questions about a friend or family member",
    "Reading Part 5 — open cloze (grammar gaps)"
  ],
  "why": "This is the number one A1 mistake: forgetting the -s for he, she and it, or adding it where it doesn't belong. Get this right and every description of a friend, a pet or a teacher — <i>She trains every day, he doesn't like maths</i> — sounds correct straight away.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "At the football pitch, Sofia watches her friend. \"Nadia <b>plays</b> really well!\" she says. \"She <b>doesn't stop</b> running. Look, she <b>scores</b> a goal! <b>Does</b> she <b>train</b> every day?\" \"Yes,\" says Mateo, \"she <b>trains</b> every morning before school.\"",
    "ask": "Find three verbs with an extra letter at the end, the negative with doesn't, and the question with Does."
  },
