          ],
          "note": "short answer"
        }
      ],
      "caption": "The helper <b>does</b> already carries the -s, so the verb goes back to <i>play</i> in questions and negatives."
    },
    {
      "type": "spelling",
      "title": "Adding -s for he, she, it",
      "rules": [
        {
          "rule": "Most verbs: + s",
          "examples": [
            "play → play<b>s</b>",
            "run → run<b>s</b>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "rule": "-s, -sh, -ch, -x, -o: + es",
          "examples": [
            "watch → watch<b>es</b>",
            "go → go<b>es</b>",
            "do → do<b>es</b>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "rule": "consonant + y: y → ies",
          "examples": [
            "study → stud<b>ies</b>",
            "try → tr<b>ies</b>"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "caption": "Say the verb out loud — if it's hard to say with just -s, it probably needs -es. One verb breaks every rule: have → <b>has</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "steps",
      "title": "How to make a question with does",
      "steps": [
        {
          "label": "Start with Does",
          "ex": "<b>Does</b>"
        },
        {
          "label": "Add the person",
          "ex": "Does <b>she</b>"
        },
        {
          "label": "Add the verb with NO -s",
          "ex": "Does she <b>play</b> tennis"
        },
        {
          "label": "Add the question mark",
          "ex": "Does she play tennis<b>?</b>"
        }
      ],
      "caption": "No <i>do</i> is needed with does. Does already means he/she/it, so the verb loses its -s."
    }
  ],
